{
  "id": "binary-tree-level-order-traversal",
  "questionId": "binary-tree-level-order-traversal",
  "slug": "binary-tree-level-order-traversal",
  "title": "Binary Tree Level Order Traversal",
  "difficulty": "Easy",
  "category": "Tree bfs",
  "patterns": [
    "tree bfs",
    "queue"
  ],
  "lists": [
    "blind-75",
    "grind-75",
    "interview-150"
  ],
  "hints": [
    "you need to know where one level ends and the next begins - what if you processed the queue in fixed-size batches?",
    "before draining nodes off the queue for a level, check the queue's current length - that's exactly how many nodes belong to this level",
    "for each of those nodes, record its value, then push its valid (in-bounds, non-sentinel) children onto the queue for the next round",
    "stop once the queue is empty; the level list you built during each round becomes one row of the answer"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "pushing sentinel or out-of-bounds child indices onto the queue instead of skipping them",
    "not snapshotting the queue length before the inner loop, causing nodes from the next level to bleed into the current one",
    "using a plain DFS but forgetting to group by depth, producing a flat list instead of a list of levels"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "A binary tree is given to you as a plain integer array named `tree`, using a complete-binary-tree (heap) index layout:\n\n- the root sits at index 0\n- a node at index `i` has its left child at index `2*i+1` and right child at index `2*i+2`\n- a child index only counts as real if it's within the array **and** its value isn't the sentinel `-101`, which means \"there is no node here\"\n\nFor example, `[1, 2, 3, -101, 4]` encodes this tree:\n\n```\n      1\n     / \\\n    2   3\n     \\\n      4\n```\n\nNode 2's left child is missing and its right child is 4.\n\n**Group the tree's node values level by level, from the root's level down to the deepest level, with each level's values listed left to right. Return the levels as a list of lists.**\n",
    "constraints": [
      "1 <= tree.length <= 2000",
      "-100 <= tree[i] <= 100 for every real node value",
      "tree[i] == -101 marks a missing node and never appears as a real value"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "a single-node tree",
      "a tree that is a straight left-leaning or right-leaning chain (one node per level)",
      "a tree missing an entire subtree (e.g. root has only a left child)"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "tree = [3, 9, 20, -101, -101, 15, 7]",
        "output": "[[3], [9, 20], [15, 7]]",
        "expectedOutput": "[[3], [9, 20], [15, 7]]",
        "explanation": "root 3 is level 0; 9 and 20 are level 1; 15 and 7 are level 2 (children of 20, since 9's children are both sentinels)"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_2",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "tree = [1]",
        "output": "[[1]]",
        "expectedOutput": "[[1]]",
        "explanation": "a single node is its own, only level"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_3",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "tree = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7]",
        "output": "[[1], [2, 3], [4, 5, 6, 7]]",
        "expectedOutput": "[[1], [2, 3], [4, 5, 6, 7]]",
        "explanation": "a perfectly complete tree of 3 levels"
      }
    ],
